times even here in church it's  bright with artificial light and the  stars are hidden from us in the  wilderness can be a place of clarity  then again the wilderness is not a place  most of us desire to go or at least stay  for very long the wilderness of mental  illness or physical pain the wilderness  of isolation or grief these are the  landscapes in which we feel most  particularly alone when we run out of  our own resources and find ourselves  parched yet this is the very place where  Christ meets us John the Baptist assures  us in the wilderness that Christ is not  far off he is coming and in the meantime  John is showing us how to find scarce  water in the desert pointing away toward  hope when the landscape seems hopeless  we are turning into this second week of  Advent are you in the wilderness yet  Fred need nura writes the holy way of  Advent that cross toting fools tread  leads not out of the wilderness but ever  deeper into it none of us will escape  the wilderness anyway our whole life  happens within its wordless void on its  arid slopes that drain the life from us  as we wander in circles learning the  same lessons over and over there in the  wilderness as we wander in those circles  John the Baptist call  out to us to reflect to confess to  repent reflect on the wilderness in  which we find ourselves this Advent  confess how we wrongly assumed we could  travel with just a little bit of water  and our own self sufficiency would feel  less the rest of the way repent because  we set out alone and now we see we just  might need that holy company that is  always with us I don't know about you  but I can't seem to remember what I  learned in the wilderness sure I learned  my lesson but amnesia sets in about two  minutes later and I find myself  wandering around the wilderness again  thirsty maybe life's goal shouldn't be  to avoid the wilderness those hard times  those unforgiving landscapes but instead  practice living Christ sway in the  wilderness in 